92186045417 true] [17592186411555 1077 "selection" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411555 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411536 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411555 1079 #uuid "d6e2ae69-22b8-5724-b660-3eef8661b061" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411476 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411482 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411495 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411512 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411522 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1077 "selection" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411495 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411556 1079 #uuid "dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411468 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411469 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411489 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411501 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411539 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1077 "selection" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411468 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411557 1079 #uuid "e3ae2aad-9d53-58ac-93a0-f36b60a4bfa8" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411473 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411485 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411510 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411523 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411530 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1077 "selection" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411530 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411558 1079 #uuid "e65c8161-0653-52f7-980c-c7f61d6aeb33" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411559 1075 #edb/ref 17592186411475 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411559 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411559 1077 "field-1" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411559 1078 #edb/ref 17592186411475 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411559 1079 #uuid "e6dd545c-98d8-56db-ace2-b43058497897" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411545 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1069 "q-89399" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1071 "It is known that $41%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1072 #uuid "265e293f-7554-5958-abfd-df783c671c82" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411560 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people with blood type O+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ trials\n\n- the probability of finding a person with blood type O+ is\n\n$$\np=0.41\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.41).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(1-0.41)}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(0.59)}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a person with blood type O+) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2}){(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3}){(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & ={(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+10{(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+45{(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+120{(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & =0.358\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411550 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1069 "q-130357" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam in a class of $25$ students\n- The number of cards drawn until a red card is obtained\n- The number of books published by a publishing house in a month" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1072 #uuid "29a87319-221e-52be-a49a-7a7f42765bfa" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411561 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411554 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1069 "q-84748" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1072 #uuid "46c22425-ee4e-5a56-9d8f-8f1ffa9ada86" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411562 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411543 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1069 "q-331491" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1071 "A bag contains $5$ green marbles, $7$ yellow marbles, and $8$ black marbles. An experiment of randomly selecting one marble from the bag and putting it back is repeated $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a black marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1072 #uuid "48a19d94-de64-5ea0-b742-40601b9c76a4" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411563 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a black marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ selections.\n\n- The probability of selecting a black marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{5+7+8}=\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.276,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411558 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1069 "q-130748" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1071 "$70%$ of union members are in favor of a specific change to their conditions of employment. A random sample of $6$ members is taken. What is the probability that at most $2$ members are in favor of the changed conditions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1072 #uuid "69ccd3d4-6bc6-5b97-931d-42636fcb0b40" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411564 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of members in the sample in favor of the change.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ members\n\n- since $70%$ of union members are in favor of the change, the probability of selecting a member in favor of the change is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(1-0.7)}^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most two members are in favor of the changed conditions. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}){(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}){(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{6-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{6-2} \\\\  & =1{(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6}+6{(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{5}+15{(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.070\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411557 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1069 "q-130880" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1071 "An infectious flu virus is spreading through a school. The probability of a randomly selected student having the flu next week is $0.3.$ If a class has $25$ students, what is the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1072 #uuid "6d707ab1-b369-599a-9705-58f642e1bb10" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411565 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of students having the flu next week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=25$ students in the class\n\n- the probability of selecting a student having the flu next week is $0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(25,0.3).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(1-0.3)^{25-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{25-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=25)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{25-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25}+25(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{24}] \\\\  & =1-0.001\\,571 \\\\  & ≈0.998\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411549 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1069 "q-84807" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1071 "In a bag containing candies, there is a $70%$ chance of getting a red candy when a random candy is taken. If $16$ candies are removed from the bag, what is the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\nYou may assume that the bag contains a large number of candies and that the probability of selecting a red candy is the same on each trial." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1072 #uuid "7a3cf3a8-b182-51ac-8e52-79ae8f41f3ac" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411566 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of red candies.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ candies\n\n- the probability of getting a red candy is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red. This corresponds to $P(X<15).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X<15)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<15) & =1-P(X\\ge 15) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=15)+P(X=16)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{15})(0.7)^{15}(0.3)^{16-15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{16})(0.7)^{16}(0.3)^{16-16}] \\\\  & =1-[16(0.7)^{15}(0.3)+(0.7)^{16}] \\\\  & =1-0.026\\,112 \\\\  & =0.974\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411542 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1069 "q-84747" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of times \"head\" is obtained when a coin is tossed $10$ times\n- The number of students in a class\n- The number of tickets sold for a concert in $3$ days" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1072 #uuid "8dd54609-ae0c-53a9-a2a1-7823f9aa6595" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411567 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of \"heads\" in $10$ tosses can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students in a class cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of tickets sold for a concert cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411555 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1069 "q-89380" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1071 "A fair $5$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $5$ is rolled $9$ times. What is the probability of getting a $1$ or a $2$ exactly five times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1072 #uuid "8e0e75d6-02e9-556c-b809-4824d53487ed" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411568 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $1$ or a $2$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=9$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $1$ or $2$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(9,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{5}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{9-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!(9-5)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =126{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =0.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411552 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1069 "q-89398" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1071 "A basketball player practices shooting $10$ free throws. The player has an $80%$ chance of making a basket in each free throw. What is the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1072 #uuid "90500394-6fc0-5ba4-a5dc-ea52dd5425de" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411569 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of baskets that the player makes.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ free throws\n\n- the probability of making a basket in each free throw is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.8)^{9}(0.2)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.8)^{10}(0.2)^{0} \\\\  & =10(0.8)^{9}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{10} \\\\  & =0.376\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411548 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1069 "q-331962" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1071 "A drone has a $65%$ chance of successfully delivering a package. It makes $5$ deliveries in one day. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it succeeds at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1072 #uuid "a48cf853-bbc5-5226-99de-0617e7d6b622" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411570 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful deliveries.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ deliveries.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=65%=0.65.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.65).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(1-0.65)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(0.35)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the drone succeeds at most $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.65)^{4}(0.35)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.65)^{5}(0.35)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.65)^{4}(0.35)+(0.65)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.428\\,415 \\\\  & ≈0.572,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411553 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1069 "q-130882" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1071 "$5%$ of electric bulbs are defective at manufacture. If six bulbs are randomly tested, with each one being replaced before the next is chosen, what is the probability that at least two are defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1072 #uuid "b1efc3f5-91c0-5d80-b7bf-74abaff81c0a" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411571 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of defective tested bulbs. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ tested bulbs\n\n- the probability of choosing a defective bulb is $p=5%=0.05$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.05).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(1-0.05)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(0.95)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least two bulbs are defective. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{6-1}] \\\\  & =1-[1(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6}+6(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.967\\,226 \\\\  & ≈0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411547 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1069 "q-331492" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1071 "A bag contains $9$ blue marbles, $5$ red marbles, and $6$ green marbles. A marble is drawn at random and replaced before the next draw. This process is repeated $7$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a blue marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1072 #uuid "c93c3864-ab3f-5794-a142-3eab6e62d56b" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411572 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a blue marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ trials.\n\n- The probability of selecting a blue marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{9+5+6}=\\frac{9}{20}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{9}{20}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3}){(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!}{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =35{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(\\frac{11}{20})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.292,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411546 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1069 "q-131048" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1071 "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $3:2.$ Ten chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1072 #uuid "cfd01f57-c0cd-576d-bb6c-c4a4c9533c2d" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411573 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $3:2,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{3}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{3}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 9).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 9)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=9)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =1-P(X>9) \\\\  & =1-P(X=10) \\\\  & =1-(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =1-1{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0} \\\\  & =1-0.006\\,047 \\\\  & ≈0.994\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411556 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1069 "q-130361" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of trees in a forest of $300$ trees that have bird's nests\n- The number of vaccines administered in a country during one season\n- The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1072 #uuid "e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411574 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of trees that have bird's nests among $300$ trees can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $300$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although receiving a vaccine can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411559 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1069 "q-331525" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1071 "A basketball player has a $50%$ chance of making a free throw. If they take $7$ shots, what is the probability that they make at most $3$ of them?\n\nThe probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1072 #uuid "ec42c1b6-69ea-5d9d-b8b5-5ad8c82e5789" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411575 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ shots.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=50%=0.5.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.5).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $3$ or fewer of the $7$ shots are successful. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\sum }_{x=0}^{3}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})](0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =(1+7+21+35)\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411551 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1069 "q-84797" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1071 "On a particular webpage, there is an $80%$ probability that each downloaded file does not contain a computer virus. If $6$ files are downloaded, what is the probability that at least $5$ of the files are virus-free? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1072 #uuid "f0df1382-8e7a-5174-b609-3e041c2744d5" 13194139534133 17592186045417 true] [17592186411576 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of virus-free files.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ files\n\n- the probability that each file is virus-free is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $5$ of the $6$ files are virus-free. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.8)^{5}(0.2)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.8)^{5}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & =0.655\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139534133 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" 17592186411468 "answer-0dfd0d54-03a3-56e8-826f-a80849a9aeef" 17592186411469 "answer-0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" 17592186411470 "answer-13359ee6-0e60-5eae-8685-260358b6d02a" 17592186411471 "answer-16e1f5c3-3735-584e-8765-6dea06b02e2e" 17592186411472 "answer-177c5f24-3832-5fee-961f-92a28b65ff5f" 17592186411473 "answer-181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" 17592186411474 "answer-18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" 17592186411475 "answer-1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" 17592186411476 "answer-21ae41fa-236b-530c-90e6-fb5cf9abc0e0" 17592186411477 "answer-22ff577d-7f26-5b83-b643-9fce2c24c06e" 17592186411478 "answer-24186a4f-baf5-5bbf-8437-a6d633e7ff67" 17592186411479 "answer-247eb43d-feb9-5cee-9894-cb5032551bdc" 17592186411480 "answer-26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" 17592186411481 "